    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1137">

                <pb facs="0941" n="931" xml:id="pg931"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.351/10–2549: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Korea</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Muccio</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Seoul</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-10-25T15:00:00+09:00">October 25, 1949—3
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 25—4:42 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1323. From Paddock: Reference last paragraph Deptel 898, October 24 to
                        Seoul.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d1137-f1"/> I sent plain language telegram
                    October 21 from Dairen through usual Soviet Consulate General channel, saying we
                    embarking that day. Soon as ship left harbor, I sent telegram to American
                    Embassy, Seoul, to relay to Department (radio operator said this fastest method)
                    stating we had embarked without difficulties and requesting Department notify
                    interested posts Consulate closed as of October 21. Now find Embassy never
                    received this telegram. [Paddock.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Muccio</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
